is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce an inter-floor connectivity failure. End-to-end tests show normal reachability across the “big switch” aggregation and between floors/VLANs. From a downstairs host (pc1, 10.5.0.1) I successfully pinged multiple upstairs hosts across different floors/VLANs: VLAN10 (pc5–pc8), VLAN20 (pc9–pc11), and VLAN30 (pc12–pc14) with 0% loss and millisecond RTTs. Bidirectional checks also succeeded: hosts on VLAN10 and VLAN30 reached pc1 and pc2 without loss. The topology-wide probe likewise showed clean connectivity between disparate floors (e.g., dc_pc5→pc10/pc11, pc12/13/14→pc10/11). pc1’s network config and default route (via 10.5.0.254) are correct, indicating inter-VLAN routing is functioning.

Component status: the aggregation switch (mswitch0) uplinks to access switches (switch1–4) are passing traffic; no evidence of VLAN isolation, trunk failure, or loop/STP instability during testing. Mechanistically, if an aggregation trunk or VLAN tagging were broken, we would see consistent ping failures between floors; instead we observed 0% loss.

Localization: No specific device/interface shows fault; traffic traverses mswitch0 and access switches normally.

Root cause: Not reproducible at this time (likely transient or client-specific).

Corrective fix: No changes made. Please have the affected user retry. If the issue recurs, capture exact time, source/destination IPs, and we will check switch port/trunk state and logs at that moment. === END ===